{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00420 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Promazine, a tricyclic antidepressant, is implicated in the treatment of Vasomotor Rhinitis through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Promazine may exert a modulatory effect on the immune system, as it is associated with indications such as Atopic Conjunctivitis and Potassium Iodide, both of which are linked to Vasomotor Rhinitis. The mechanism of action is not directly stated, but the presence of these indications implies a potential role in reducing inflammation or mast cell activation, which are common in allergic rhinitis. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of support for this relationship, though the evidence is not strong enough to definitively establish a causal link. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Promazine to influence vascular tone and inflammatory responses, though further mechanistic studies are needed to confirm its role in Vasomotor Rhinitis.",
